WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280
Mega Man!

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:06.660
Will you and Mega Man penetrate the seven separate societies of Dr.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.440
Wily and preserve the human race?

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.340
You're in control!

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:33.840
Nostalgia! And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.000
I'm Mike.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.440
I'm Joe. I'm Sean.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.380
I'm Sam. Guys, how are we doing?

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:39.780
I'm alright. Excellent. Alright.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.440
Welcome to Monsteropolis.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.400
Population? Not to be confused with Monstropolis.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.700
Five. Well, what's weird is that's the Monsters, Inc.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.740
world. And then Mega Man gets away with it here.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.960
That's actually the name of the place where Mega Man 1 takes place.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.400
We're talking about Mega Man 1.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:01.960
Welcome. Welcome.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.840
What do you guys know about Mega Man?

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:06.640
Mega Man? Is it a robot?

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.160
Not the guy himself.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.520
The franchise. About the franchise.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:15.000
Like, you know, Mega Man is a franchise that is just as big as, like, you know, some of the other...

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.840
Some of the other franchises that we've been covering, like Legend of Zelda, Super Mario Brothers.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.440
Mega Man's been going on for a long time.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:28.740
And the direction of Mega Man has changed a lot since, like, the beginning of the games.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:34.440
Of the games, like, it started off as one thing, and now it's like there's, like, five different Mega Man franchises.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.640
And people are just clamoring for, like, more of the old.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.340
So, I'm just curious what you guys know.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.060
Joe, you want to tell us what you know? Yeah, I mean, honestly, I'm aware of Mega Man in, like, my peripherals.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.360
Like, I know it's out there.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:47.900
I've seen it. I've definitely played one of the NES games when I was a kid.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.460
Not this one. I have played it before.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.800
But when I was a kid, I had one I would pick up once in a while, but never got very far.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.440
Otherwise, though, it's just kind of been in the background of my life.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:01:59.620
Okay. Until now.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.680
Yeah, it's hard to be a video gamer and not be aware of its existence.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.360
Sean, what do you got for us?

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.240
I was introduced to Mega Man with Mega Man 8 on the PlayStation.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.320
My stepbrothers were really into it.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:19.180
That was the one that had all of the terrible voiceover work in the anime sections.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.680
And I didn't really like it.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:23.680
It was way too hard.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.820
And, I mean, it looked beautiful, but I was totally for like, no, I want 3D.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.040
I want 3D action.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.880
And then, you know, that sort of wore off, and then I started playing Mega Man X on Super NES.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.260
And, you know, yeah, it's been there.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.180
I like playing as him in Smash.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.240
I mean, it's here and there.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.140
Not as peripheral as Joe, but I'm not a huge fan.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:52.880
Okay. Sam?

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.580
I'm kind of the same as Joe.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.540
I, you know, obviously knew about Mega Man, just for being a video game fan in general.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:12.640
But I feel like I may have played the first one or one of the NES ones in some kind of, you know, as like some mini game and some other game or somehow, but never more than just a couple minutes of it.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.760
And I really just know him mostly from Smash 4 and Smash Ultimate.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:22.780
Okay. And I know that people say he like shoots lemons, which is like a joke.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.880
I see it. But besides that, I don't really, I didn't really know anything going into it.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:38.280
All right. And for me, I was playing, as a kid, I was playing the Sonic Mega Collection on GameCube in like 2002, I want to say.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.380
I had just finished Sonic Adventure 2 Battle for GameCube.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.980
And I was like, oh, I would like to play more Sonic games.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.500
So they had the Sonic Mega Collection, which takes you through that whole history.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.040
And I love those games.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:51.600
It made me fall in love with Sonic because I never had Sega consoles growing up.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.340
And then I heard about the Mega Man Anniversary Collection.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.760
And I was like, oh, well, I like the Sonic Collection so much.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.480
I'm sure I'll love the Mega Man version of this whole thing.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.420
And I got that for GameCube as well.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.740
And I just remember thinking like, wow, this is sadistic.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:11.240
Like, you know, I was young, but like I'm used to like playing a certain kind of game.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.100
And Mega Man is the kind of game that you need like, you need to think about what you're going to do.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.080
You can't really run through this game.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.800
Yeah, exactly. You need to think about how you're going to deal with the enemies on the current screen.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.780
And then what's your plan of attack when you enter the new screen.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:29.700
It's kind of like the complete opposite of Sonic where you're just, you're always on a new screen.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.040
And so you're just going in guns blazing all the time.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.920
Right, right. Yeah. And so it was just so different to me that I was like, this is really hard.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.000
I don't enjoy this at all.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.320
I'm done with Mega Man.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.880
And I was done for a very long time.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.920
And then I started collecting NES games.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.220
And I had Mega Man 1 and 2.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.360
And I think I also played 4, which is the one that adds like the sliding mechanic.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.160
No, no. It adds the charge shot.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:53.720
The charge shot. That's very controversial.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.260
We'll get to that. We'll cross that bridge when we get there.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.100
Okay. But right now, since we're talking about Mega Man 1, let's do some first impressions.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:05.920
Sam? I honestly just kind of felt like I thought it would.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.700
You know, you jump, you shoot, you shoot enemies.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.820
It's colorful. The music was nice.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.660
But Mega Man had always been in your peripheral, right?

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.460
Like you said, you haven't really played one.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:24.140
So going in and having the very first idea of Mega Man being your first experience to do it too,

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:30.740
did you feel like, did it meet the hype of a franchise that's been going on for 30 years?

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:38.380
Yeah, I think so. I think it kind of, you know, it felt a little bit stiff, but kind of like NES stiff, if that makes sense.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.660
Not like it wasn't bad or anything.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:46.140
Just that it was, you know, it just kind of, it has just aged a little bit and you can tell.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.360
But for what it is, it was pretty much what I expected.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:06:00.320
Cool. Sean? When I first started playing it, because I had Mega Man 8 and X in my, you know, mental backlog,

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:06.360
it just sort of surprised me how much of the game has sort of stayed the same.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:13.600
Like the feeling of the game, I mean, every single one of them is just run, jump, and shoot with a couple variations.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:20.760
But like, you warp in, like every game is just like, you warp in, that's got like a feel to it.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.080
The shooting still feels the same.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:31.660
It's just like, it has like the essentials ever since the first one.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.120
8 feels like 1, which feels like X, which feels like X4.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:40.000
Everything has this very Mega Man quality to it, whether it's one game or the other.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:41.240
So I just found that interesting.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.280
That was my first impression was, this is Mega Man.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:50.960
Alright, Joe? The first thing that like really stood out to me was that this is what I imagine when I think Nintendo hard.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:58.860
This is my maybe not always true idea of what Nintendo hard is, where it's like, it's hard, but not like super, super cheap hard.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:00.400
Like hard where I can feel like I can learn it.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:04.820
So even for, no matter which level you start from, I feel like I, you have to learn the level.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:09.380
And it's really, it presents more of a challenge, more of a fair challenge than I think we've had in a lot of games.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:10.440
I agree with that.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:13.200
I had a similar, similar thought about it being Nintendo hard.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:20.580
Right, and I think it's like the, our like idealistic memories of what Nintendo hard is, where it's like not really annoying.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.660
Like very challenging, but doable as well.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:25.520
Right, right. And not like cheap doable.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:33.100
Exactly. Yeah, and so I played the game before, but I didn't want to think about the future of Mega Man games.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:38.720
I just wanted to focus on like, okay, this is my first time playing Mega Man 1, 4, Nostalgia.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.340
Like, how do I feel about it right now?

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:45.420
Um, and I, you know, we talked about how you need a lot of focus and direction in this game.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:51.020
And I learned that like a lot by restarting my game very often.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:55.300
There's a lot of times where I'd be like two bosses in or maybe something like that.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:58.520
Like I'd have two of the power-ups and I got to another one and then I died there.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:01.220
And I was just like, you know, maybe I just need to start from scratch again.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:07.840
And I can't tell you how many times I did that just because I felt like I wasn't, I wasn't getting how to play the game properly.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:13.940
There was a moment for me where this game finally clicked for me and I was like, oh, that's how you play a Mega Man game.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:17.100
You can't just like accept the damage and move on.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.560
Like there is a way, and it's designed by the programmers.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.040
Like there is a way to navigate your way through this.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:27.260
It's not like an enemy was placed somewhere and you're just supposed to accept.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:30.880
Yeah, you're supposed to get hit or supposed to like deal with it.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:31.900
It's like, you know.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:33.020
It's a lot of learning.

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:41.680
Yeah, a lot of learning. So I think like my first impression was just that I really need to like take my time and not think about like, okay, I'm on this screen right now and I need to get to the next screen immediately.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:48.080
Well, were there any actual long-term repercussions for losing all your lives?

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.360
Because I feel like it just wasn't.

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:51.220
So this is completely psychological.

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:53.460
Psychological for me. The score resets, right?

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:59.500
Starting over and just like being like, you know what, let's just start from the beginning and maybe try a different order of bosses.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:08.620
You know, there is a weakness pattern that we'll talk about, but just getting around that and almost like you said, psychologically, like learning how to play this game.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:14.160
Yeah. Because the first thing that we'll mention about this game is that enemies are placed.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.760
Yes. In a very specific way.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:22.720
Finally. Yeah, but so it's like there is a specific way to play that the programmers have in mind too.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:31.980
It's very similar to Super Mario Brothers where there are times where you need to hold down the run button and jump accordingly to make a reach because they planned it to be that way.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.160
In other games that we've played, I just think about like games like Trojan or Russian Attack.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.680
Like those games, they've created the combat system.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:43.860
They've created the platforming idea, but then it's just like you just deal with it however you want to.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.440
There's no real like plan put out there.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:47.500
Enemies just spawn.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.440
It's not like a puzzle or anything. Right, exactly.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:51.900
Yeah, a puzzle is a good way to explain it.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:53.600
That's how I would describe each screen.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:54.720
Like a combat puzzle.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:56.140
Yeah. Right, yeah, which is cool.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.580
But before we get there, we should talk about the objective.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:05.640
For those of you that don't know what Mega Man's all about, you have to work your way through six stages in any order you choose.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:09.200
In later games, it'll become eight and that becomes the standard for how many bosses.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:12.280
But in this one, there's just six robot masters to take on.

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:20.200
You go to their stage, you defeat them, you get their weapon or their version of their power up and Mega Man equips that.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.520
And now you can take that new power up into all the other stages.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:26.800
You defeat all six to get to the evil Dr.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:34.360
Wily. And then that's where you can now be the fully powered up Mega Man where you have access to all the power ups you need in order to beat the game.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.280
Now, there's a lot to unpack there.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:37.960
Absolutely. Let's unpack it.

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:44.580
So, I mean, first I would just like to clarify to anybody that doesn't know what a Mega Man is, it's a side-scrolling platformer.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:55.820
But other than that, I think this is the first game that introduces a choose-your-own-adventure sort of situation where you can go to these levels in any order.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:59.680
And it's not just because there's a hundred levels.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:00.800
They're not even dead.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:06.000
It actually affects, it actually has an impact if you decide to go to the fire level before you go to the ice level.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:10.100
You're going to get a different weapon added to your arsenal, too.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:14.820
Where it's like, oh, maybe that would be helpful if I went to this level first and got what I needed from there before I go to this level.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:19.480
Yeah, there's strategy involved. Because each of the power ups does do things to the stage themselves.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:21.720
The bosses have a particular weakness.

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:23.880
And also it affects the enemies in different ways, too.

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:27.400
So I think, yeah, there's definitely an advantage to that kind of thing.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:34.420
But also, you know, with the way that you take down the stages in any particular order, the game doesn't tell you the order.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:38.280
It starts you on Cutman, but he's not necessarily where you want to start.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:41.520
You can immediately just choose whichever boss you want.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:45.120
And it's interesting that they're just represented by their robot master.

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:48.120
It's not like the stages have names or anything like that or places.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.580
That's kind of all additional information you can find elsewhere.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:54.420
But it's just like, which of these bosses do you want to take on?

00:11:54.540 --> 00:12:00.080
And it's almost like if you don't read the manual and you just get greeted with that screen, you have no idea what's about to happen.

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:01.780
You're like, am I playing as Cutman?

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.220
Yeah. Now that I select him?

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:10.040
Well, this is another example, not to go off topic, but this is another example of where all the context comes from the manual.

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.480
Like, there's no story element in this game.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:16.740
It's just you have to go back to the manual to understand what you're even doing.

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:25.760
So I think that if somebody were to play without the manual and you're just greeted by this, yeah, you might not think that you may think you're playing as somebody else.

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:31.980
It's very strange. I'm glad you mentioned the manual, Sean, because I think it's time for a little Tales from the Manual section.

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:43.500
So there's two, you know, we always bring up funny things in the manual, and I'm going to do that first.

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:58.600
So there's two things I definitely want to talk about here, and the first being that in the helpful game playing hints, which is literally page one after all the information goes through, it's like, before we talk about what enemies are or how to do anything, here's just game playing hints.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:00.280
Let me tell you how the health bar works.

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:06.560
Right, right, exactly. So they refer to the Mega Man energy meter as a thermometer.

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:09.940
And I'm like, I never once thought of it like that.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:12.440
You know, they're talking about that thing on the left-hand side of your screen.

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:17.680
I know what you mean, but this might be the first time we've seen a vertical health meter.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:21.000
And, like, maybe that's why they would use that analogy.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:25.580
I know that, like, to us, it just sounds like a stupid, like, uh...

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:26.780
Mistranslation or something.

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:28.760
Mistranslation or just redundant information.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.540
But I guess, yeah, I don't know.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:39.880
But also, like, referring to it as a thermometer, and this is completely, like, just semantics, but, like, referring to it as a thermometer makes me think that you wouldn't want to have a full bar, because that means you have, like, a fever.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.600
You're a robot, though, so you want to be, like...

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:44.820
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, you want to have gas.

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:46.000
Maximum output. Yeah.

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:51.360
So I just thought that was funny that they describe it as thermometer-shaped, but point taken, Sean.

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:59.380
You're correct. And then there is, like, immediately they have to call it out, because they just never planned a way to deal with this otherwise.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:00.800
And it's really silly.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:03.440
But they have to tell you about the magnet beam.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:06.900
Right. Because if you don't find out about it, you won't be able to beat the game.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:10.260
Yeah. But it's also not, like, mandatory to ever, like, a game.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:11.180
I missed it.

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:16.240
I did, too. And then I read the manual, and I did think to myself, they put that right there so they can be, like...

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:18.060
It was in the first page of the manual.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.320
Yeah. It's good to me. So I was like, okay, fair enough.

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:24.620
I just think it's insane that that's the best way that they could think to communicate that.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:27.180
And I get that, like, back then, games were about secrets.

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:29.120
But that's not really a secret.

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:32.800
No. That's not really a secret. Like, just make another stage where you fight Magnet Man.

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:34.860
You know? You're fighting Cut Man.

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:36.080
You're fighting Bomb Man.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:38.080
Might as well fight Magnet Man and get that power.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:41.820
It honestly does feel like afterwards when they playtested it, they were like, you know what?

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:44.140
This magnet thing, it kind of sucks the way we have it.

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:46.080
But it's too late to do anything else in the game.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:50.500
Like, just throw it in the manual on the front page so everyone knows they get the magnet beam.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:54.220
Not to mention that magnet beam is, like, practically broken in the game.

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:56.000
But we'll talk about that in the power-up section.

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:59.380
But I appreciate Magnum Beam's inclusion in this game.

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:02.100
Me too. I think the more power-ups, the merrier in these Mega Man games.

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:04.500
Like, once you become the full Mega Man.

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:10.980
Yeah. Once you become the full Mega Man, there's, like, no feeling like that in a game so far on the NES.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:12.560
But we'll get into that later.

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:13.800
The full Mega Man.

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:16.020
What would you call that Mega Man?

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:18.880
The Micro Mag...

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:20.700
The Essential Mega Man.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:22.040
Macho Man. The Essential Mega Man.

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:23.280
Macho Man. I like that better.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.780
Okay, let's call him Macho Man during that phase.

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.020
Anything else from the manual section anybody wants to talk about?

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.900
Well, we could... I mean, we have to talk about the change in...

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:34.660
The complete change in what the story is.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:36.580
Okay. You want to walk us through it?

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:39.340
Well, I mean, it's like...

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:44.400
It's just supposed to take place on Earth in the, like, Japanese game.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:47.940
And I don't know if it's just because, like, oh, we can't have...

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:50.160
We can't have... There'd be violence in the real world.

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:53.820
So we have to make up this place called Monsteropolis, like we were talking about.

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:59.100
And I don't know why that was a pertinent detail that needed to be addressed.

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.180
And then they changed the name of the Dr.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:03.400
Light to Dr.

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:06.280
Wright. And they kept Wiley.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:07.680
I don't know. Yeah.

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:10.100
It's a bunch of weird anachronisms.

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:12.780
And it just doesn't make any sense to me.

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:14.940
Yes. No, I completely agree.

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:18.760
And also, the only story you're getting from this game isn't even in the manual.

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:20.160
It's on the back of the box.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.380
So I feel like that's just something that, like...

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:25.340
You know, I don't pick up the back of the box, read through that, and say...

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:27.900
Well, there's a story section in the manual, right?

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.240
Oh, right. There's the Mega Man story thing.

00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:34.560
Yeah, yeah, yeah. And I actually thought, not knowing that it was different from the Japanese version,

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:39.280
I actually thought, like, oh, that's a pretty solid explanation of, like, a short description

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:40.160
of what's going on here.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:44.060
Yeah. Say what you will, but Monsteropolis is pretty clever.

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:45.940
Is it? It's a pretty clever meaning.

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:46.960
Oh, because monster.

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.120
Yeah. Oh, and Manopolis.

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:50.320
Like a city.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:54.640
Even though, forget that nobody is ever referred to as a monster in this game.

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:56.840
Yeah, that's why I was confused. I was like, are you the monster?

00:16:57.080 --> 00:16:58.700
They don't collect children's screams.

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:00.580
Do they know the citizens monsters?

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:02.200
The citizens are monsters.

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:04.200
The citizens name their city Monsteropolis.

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:08.140
For those of you that don't know the Mega Man story, here's how I imagine it.

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:09.300
I'm not reading the manual.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:14.060
I'm just saying, I always thought of it as, like, the human race is basically under threat

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:14.900
by Dr.

00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:18.860
Wily, who's turned all the good robots that were supposed to, like, you know, like,

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:20.000
Cutman doesn't, yeah.

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:21.020
He's a worker bot guy.

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:22.060
He's just cutting stuff.

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:25.160
And then all of a sudden, he's, like, reprogrammed to be evil.

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:28.740
And now Dr. Wily's using all those robot masters to take over the Earth.

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:29.840
That's what it says in the manual.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:30.940
Okay, good. Well, except Earth.

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:32.000
But otherwise, that's what it says.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:36.780
Yeah, and then, like, you know, it's up to Mega Man, the only Android left, I guess,

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:37.800
to save the day.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:40.980
Right? Well, roll. Yeah, wait, how is that different than...

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:41.820
No, I'm saying I don't...

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:43.280
I'm saying that... I think I got that.

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:44.420
Mike wrote the manual.

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:52.240
There were just a bunch of seemingly insignificant details changed that I just found weird because

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:57.320
it's not, like, in Kid Icarus...

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.260
Cupid. The game with the...

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:03.500
Oh, yeah. Like, where it used to be a dog.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:05.120
Oh, yeah, I know what you're talking about.

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:06.700
Well, I hear that. I'm speaking of...

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:11.780
Chubby Cherub. It's not like, well, this doesn't make any sense without the manga or something like

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:15.080
that. Right. It's just, let's just change these details just because.

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:20.920
And I got why they changed Rockman to Mega Man, but I don't understand why they changed

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:22.980
the world to Monsteropolis.

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:48.680
Yes. Everyone here is surely familiar with the box art, though, for the Mega Man North American

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:49.920
version, right?

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.540
I feel like it's kind of infamous in terms of...

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:54.600
It's part of the video game canon now, I guess.

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:58.700
Yeah, yeah. It went beyond just, like, NES collecting and stuff like that.

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:04.700
It's just known as, like, the strangest translation of a game into a box art.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:10.020
So, who wants to try and describe this thing in case people don't know what they're looking

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:11.880
at? I think I got this.

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:20.560
Okay. All right. So, we got, in the background, kind of like a, you know, like an outrun sort of checkerboard.

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:25.900
And then in the foreground, there is what appears to be one of the apes from Planet of the Apes

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:33.800
dressed up in, like, a ridiculous costume that I guess is Mega Man.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:35.300
But it's blue and yellow.

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:37.040
Blue and yellow. This costume is blue and yellow.

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:38.460
And he's holding a gun.

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:39.500
Yeah, he's holding a gun.

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:40.720
Which also doesn't happen.

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:42.760
Not like the arm cannon thing that he has.

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:43.760
It's just a gun.

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:47.140
Right. And he's just in such an awkward pose.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:49.620
And he's got, like, a codpiece or something also.

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:52.500
He's in the middle of doing squats.

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:55.980
His position is, like, you know in Spider-Man 3 when he does that dance?

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:58.380
Like, he's doing the Spider-Man 3 dance at the end.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:01.560
Before Spider-Man 3, he was the first to do it.

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:03.840
That's probably where they got it from in Spider-Man 3.

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:06.840
His legs just don't look like they're facing the right way.

00:20:06.940 --> 00:20:11.920
Yeah. The whole piece of artwork has problems with perspective.

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:14.780
Yeah. Whether it's on his head, in his arms.

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:17.200
I think they just, like, copied.

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:24.260
They must have, like, traced a sketch of someone's face from, like, a noir detective novel or something.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:29.480
And just put it under this really weird, like, he's not facing the right direction.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:31.880
He looks like he's riding an invisible horse.

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:40.460
And so the real story here is that Mega Man, they were working on it for a while over at Capcom.

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:43.620
And then they weren't sure, like, how it was going to be received or whatever.

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:45.980
But it was selling fairly well in Japan.

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:49.440
And so they immediately decided, like, we need to get this thing out to North America, like, stat.

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:57.460
And they gave the, like, in-house designer one day to make the new box art for Mega Man 1.

00:20:57.580 --> 00:20:58.860
Which is still terrible.

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:02.560
Okay? Knowing that you had a full day and you still brought this.

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:10.220
Exactly. This is, I could see this being an okay piece of work if you had maybe, like, a half hour.

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:13.280
And you were like, well, I messed up that perspective.

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:15.180
So I gotta run with it.

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:17.680
There's so much detail in the city behind him, though.

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:19.660
Is there, though? Is there really?

00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:22.180
Not detail. There's a lot of stuff going on.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:24.960
You know? And none of that happens in the game, though.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:29.680
Yeah. Why is that all there when there's this little monkey man in the foreground?

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:34.260
The only thing I really remember, and I don't remember if it, I think it's a Lechman stage.

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:38.020
The stage with the yellow globes.

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:38.900
Yeah, that's a Lechman.

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:41.140
Oh. On the, in the background.

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:43.640
Oh, okay. I think that's a Lechman, yeah.

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:46.820
And that's what I thought of as, like, oh, that's what people live in.

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:49.160
These giant, like, yellow globe towers.

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:51.240
Could be. Nowhere to be found in the box office.

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:52.540
The box office.

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:54.220
The movie never came out.

00:21:55.520 --> 00:22:00.180
But speaking of movies, the Mega Man European box art.

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:02.900
Now that looks like a movie I would watch.

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:04.000
I don't know.

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:05.360
It looks like cosplay.

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:06.640
Sean, take us through it.

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:23.700
So the Mega Man PAL release is a very, a very dramatic movie poster, pretty much, which has a photorealistic Mega Man who has de-aged 30 years since his American version.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:28.080
It's Mega Boy. He's now not wearing metal or anything like that.

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:37.400
He's just wearing a jumpsuit with, at least they got the, the power shooter laser glove, right?

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:38.380
Yeah, the arm cannon.

00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:40.120
The arm cannon is the word.

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:48.400
And then they've got really burly versions of the, the, the masters, the robot masters in the background.

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:50.220
And then you have like Dr.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:57.380
Wily's castle in the back and then you have like the Darth Vader face in the background of a very pensive Dr.

00:22:57.540 --> 00:22:59.360
Wily. Yeah, he doesn't even really look evil.

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:02.220
He looks like he's like the mentor of.

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:03.920
There's something about like Dr.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:07.340
Wily in that top left, like, you know, and, and how he looks.

00:23:07.480 --> 00:23:11.100
And then the lightning striking on the top of Wily's tower.

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:12.620
It's very Kairoskuro.

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:18.580
I was just thinking like maybe even a little bit like Rocky Horror or like Frankenstein's castle, you know?

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:19.640
It's very Frankenstein.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:22.360
Yeah, you know, like, like what, what are we trying to get from here?

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:30.780
But I will tell you, love the way that, um, Gutman, um, Fireman and, um, Electman look in this box art.

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:36.180
Yeah. Like that's pretty cool that they did like, forget, say what you will about the photorealistic versions of Wily and Mega Man.

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:37.400
They look really cool.

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:42.120
I still think that at least they, at least it looks like it's for the right game.

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:43.600
Yes, that's true.

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:47.300
There's no, there's not a lot of, um, also wait, but who's that arm?

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:49.820
I was just going to say that. I just noticed that at the same moment you did.

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:52.380
Yeah, I was looking around like, wait, whose arm is that?

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:55.320
Yeah, there's an arm underneath behind the Mega Man logo.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:59.260
There's like a, it looks like one of the other henchmen or one of the other robot masters' arms.

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:03.360
I mean, it was probably like a bigger piece of artwork that they cropped to fit.

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:04.880
Yeah, maybe it was Bomb Man.

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:07.880
Maybe. Who's like, yeah, who just got defeated or something.

00:24:08.020 --> 00:24:12.780
I don't know. Maybe his arm is like winding up because the other one's even lower with a bomb and he's

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:15.000
about to like, heave it up there.

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:17.800
Yeah, heave it over his head. You know? But he's just trying to counterbalance the bomb.

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:18.980
I think that's it.

00:24:19.220 --> 00:24:20.220
Yeah. That's gotta be it.

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:21.160
What else could there be?

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:27.760
And so since we've talked about the American and European box arts, we might as well mention

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:32.160
that while the Famicom one is fine, it has the cartoon versions of them from the manual,

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:33.740
that's not Mega Man.

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:35.020
That is Rock Man.

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:38.040
Yeah, but it is the same guy.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:41.480
No, no, no, but I'm saying it's Rock Man is the name that he's always been.

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:43.200
He's never referred to as Mega Man in Japan.

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:44.420
They never changed that over.

00:24:44.420 --> 00:24:48.540
So you just have Rock Man as the series as it's known in Japan.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:52.980
And there's a great reason for that, which I might as well divulge into now since we've

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:57.600
talked about it. But the reason why it's called Rock Man is because this whole concept of this game is

00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:59.540
based off of rock, paper, scissors.

00:24:59.960 --> 00:25:04.140
The idea that one thing would be able to combat another thing.

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:09.580
I agree that that is a concept in the game, but I feel like it has more to do with just

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:11.920
like the musical naming conventions that they have.

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:16.620
What do you mean? Like Rock and Roll, later they have Bass and Treble.

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:18.480
Yeah, but I think that's all added afterwards.

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:21.880
Is it? Yeah, they've stated in their intentions in interviews that.

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:23.240
But Roll exists in.

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:28.360
Yeah, no, I'm saying I think that that all came after the idea of like this.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:31.520
A game based on power-ups and stuff like that.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:35.860
Oh, okay. Where basically the whole thing is just a giant rock, paper, scissors battle because, you

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:39.780
know, if ice can defeat fire, but fire can defeat.

00:25:41.060 --> 00:25:42.240
Ice. Yeah. No.

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:48.180
I know that Iceman you go to first before you go to Fireman if you're playing in the

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:53.620
correct order. But, you know, just saying that each thing has like a weakness to it, but you also have

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:55.720
that thing has a different power to the other thing.

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:57.620
So it's like this giant game of rock, paper, scissors.

00:25:57.620 --> 00:26:02.560
It's funny because it sounds weird to compare that because we're living in 2019 where that's

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:05.380
like a video game mechanic and a mechanic of a lot of things.

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:10.360
Yeah. But I guess if, you know, this hasn't really been a mechanic in much before this, I'm

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:12.520
assuming, then that's like, yeah, rock, paper, scissors.

00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:14.480
It's like you're picking what's better than the other thing.

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:18.320
It's like a better way to describe it, I guess, to people in 1987 than it is to us.

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:19.600
So, wait a second.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.460
Why isn't Cut Man the first guy that you should fight?

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:24.520
Because rock beats scissors.

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:26.540
Well, it's funny you should mention that.

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:28.580
I feel like we'll just go into development right now.

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:34.600
Cut Man is actually the first design thing for anything Mega Man related and was actually

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:35.820
going to be the protagonist.

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:37.160
He was just going to have scissors on his head.

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:41.600
Weirdly, it was going to be Cut Man, was going to be who you play as, and the idea was that

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:44.160
you were going to use his ability to...

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:48.500
But then the whole PR department was like, no running scissors, it's a bad look.

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:52.880
We'll have kids cosplaying as this guy, running around with razor blades.

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:55.600
What about when they designed Paper Man, then?

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:57.720
What happened to him? Paper Boy.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:00.700
New game. Different game. Yeah, he got changed to a different game.

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:06.800
So, in an interview with Akira Kitamura, who was the programmer on Mega Man and is,

00:27:06.800 --> 00:27:09.120
is largely responsible for the creation of Mega Man.

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:10.540
This is a great interview.

00:27:10.940 --> 00:27:13.260
He's no longer, I think, in the video game industry.

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:15.240
I think he's just done with that.

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:20.440
But a lot of people also know Inafune, who is still in the video game industry and just

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:22.140
recently put out Mighty No.

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:24.580
9. And, you know...

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:25.760
Yeah, it was there.

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:26.940
Right, it happened.

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:33.240
But a lot of people see him as the Mega Man guy, but I think that Akira is just as involved

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:35.620
in the original process of creating the Mega Man games.

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:40.660
So, when Kitamura was asked about, like, what's the process for making the Robot Masters,

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:44.780
he actually talks about that Cut Man was the first thing ever designed.

00:27:44.960 --> 00:27:50.780
He says, When thinking about Mega Man, I sometimes feel that it's not Mega Man himself, but all the

00:27:50.780 --> 00:27:54.240
different Robot Masters that are the real treasure of the series.

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:59.440
Mega Man stays the same character from game to game, but the Robot Masters are all unique

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:03.400
one-off creations with their own individual design, capabilities, and backstories.

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:08.020
It honestly wouldn't be weird at all to make one of them the star of their own games.

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:12.580
When I first created the world for Mega Man, but before I knew what the gameplay system

00:28:12.580 --> 00:28:16.240
would be, originally there was no Mega Man, no role, no Dr.

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:18.100
Light. Cut Man was the hero.

00:28:18.480 --> 00:28:23.100
I imagined a game where you'd use those scissors on his head to cut down enemies and other

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:25.260
