01:45:53.280 --> 01:45:55.420
And that's the game in a nutshell.

01:45:55.440 --> 01:45:58.120
You know, we've really gone over like every single part of it.

01:45:58.460 --> 01:46:01.700
You know, we've talked kind of how we feel about certain parts,

01:46:01.880 --> 01:46:07.780
whether it's the Robot Masters or Wily's Fortress or just like inscrutable things.

01:46:08.180 --> 01:46:09.440
The societies of Monstropolis.

01:46:09.860 --> 01:46:12.900
Yeah, the seven secret societies of Monstropolis.

01:46:13.120 --> 01:46:15.840
But how do we feel about the overall difficulty of this game?

01:46:15.980 --> 01:46:17.720
Is this Nintendo hard?

01:46:17.900 --> 01:46:19.580
Is this hard in general?

01:46:19.580 --> 01:46:21.780
Will this be hard for people today?

01:46:21.900 --> 01:46:24.100
Was it hard compared to games back then?

01:46:24.280 --> 01:46:26.000
I'm giving you a whole range of things.

01:46:26.500 --> 01:46:27.780
Just tell me what you're feeling.

01:46:27.900 --> 01:46:30.400
I'm going to say yes to all.

01:46:31.400 --> 01:46:34.020
I think it's hard on its own.

01:46:34.680 --> 01:46:36.440
I think it's Nintendo hard.

01:46:36.740 --> 01:46:40.620
But I also think in the way that it's something that can be learned,

01:46:41.280 --> 01:46:43.500
you know, and I feel like the more time that you spend with it,

01:46:43.620 --> 01:46:44.660
the easier it gets.

01:46:44.660 --> 01:46:48.700
But it's still, you know, it's still like little things will make you fail.

01:46:49.720 --> 01:46:52.120
But those are just things that you kind of get used to.

01:46:53.060 --> 01:46:55.920
Or you get used to the frustration you feel when they happen.

01:46:56.280 --> 01:46:59.280
Yeah. Because this is hard all around the board.

01:46:59.660 --> 01:47:05.260
I know that we always say that like hard games that are good are hard in a fair way.

01:47:05.260 --> 01:47:08.740
And yeah, most of the time Mega Man is hard in a fair way.

01:47:08.880 --> 01:47:11.140
But there are times where it doesn't feel fair.

01:47:11.660 --> 01:47:12.780
Yeah. Yeah, I agree.

01:47:12.980 --> 01:47:16.120
And I think like to bring up the frustration point,

01:47:16.420 --> 01:47:18.320
there was a point where I said to myself,

01:47:18.540 --> 01:47:21.160
maybe I'm just not made out for these Mega Man games.

01:47:21.180 --> 01:47:23.940
Like I remember playing the Anniversary Collection on GameCube

01:47:23.940 --> 01:47:26.020
and like I sucked at all six of them.

01:47:26.100 --> 01:47:28.200
I felt like, oh, maybe the sixth one will be easier.

01:47:28.420 --> 01:47:30.580
You know, like there's been enough time now.

01:47:30.700 --> 01:47:32.140
Maybe they've understood the mechanics.

01:47:32.520 --> 01:47:33.560
They're all just as hard.

01:47:33.560 --> 01:47:36.460
And it's like maybe that happens.

01:47:36.640 --> 01:47:40.080
But I also want to like for people who feel like they're just not good at Mega Man games,

01:47:40.140 --> 01:47:41.720
I do want to say that there's hope for you.

01:47:42.300 --> 01:47:45.400
It will be less fun than people who are just inherently good at these games.

01:47:45.540 --> 01:47:48.660
There are people out there who just like master these Mega Man games.

01:47:48.760 --> 01:47:50.580
And when like a new one comes out,

01:47:50.740 --> 01:47:52.580
they take everything they've learned from the previous ones

01:47:52.580 --> 01:47:54.140
and just kind of like roll through.

01:47:55.080 --> 01:47:58.620
But they got to just roll through all the games, you know, again and stuff like that.

01:47:58.740 --> 01:48:00.360
And that's okay too.

01:48:00.660 --> 01:48:03.440
There's nothing wrong with not being good at a particular genre.

01:48:03.560 --> 01:48:05.120
Or a particular franchise.

01:48:05.600 --> 01:48:10.040
And I think it's harder because Mega Man is like, you know,

01:48:10.140 --> 01:48:12.620
it's such a beloved franchise in video gaming.

01:48:12.800 --> 01:48:15.200
It has such a story and history that it almost feels like,

01:48:15.540 --> 01:48:17.260
well, if I want my gamer's license,

01:48:17.380 --> 01:48:19.160
I need to play the Mega Man games.

01:48:19.200 --> 01:48:20.900
You're supposed to have played them.

01:48:20.940 --> 01:48:22.020
Right, you're supposed to have played them.

01:48:22.120 --> 01:48:23.480
And it's fine either way, I think.

01:48:23.560 --> 01:48:24.980
I think it's difficult, but it's fun.

01:48:25.140 --> 01:48:26.060
Would you guys agree? Yeah.

01:48:26.400 --> 01:48:27.260
Absolutely. Cool.

01:48:33.220 --> 01:48:36.940
We've been playing throughout this episode a lot of music.

01:48:37.100 --> 01:48:40.840
Maybe more so than on any episode we've done otherwise.

01:48:41.140 --> 01:48:44.920
Like Mega Man music, I think all these tracks are just insane good.

01:48:44.920 --> 01:48:49.980
Like, I would jam out to them on my headphones without playing the game.

01:48:50.040 --> 01:48:51.640
Yeah. There's always that in, like, these games.

01:48:51.740 --> 01:48:53.500
There's always, even in the games with good music,

01:48:53.580 --> 01:48:54.700
there's always that one where it's just like,

01:48:54.720 --> 01:48:55.560
is that just noise?

01:48:55.760 --> 01:48:58.240
Or they're just like sounds, like beeps and sounds.

01:48:58.580 --> 01:49:03.460
Every single song in this game sounds like a deliberate music track.

01:49:03.460 --> 01:49:05.680
And they all have, like, length to them.

01:49:05.980 --> 01:49:09.440
Yeah. A lot of games that we've played are, like, two bars.

01:49:09.900 --> 01:49:12.060
Yeah. This is like a minute 30 on each track.

01:49:13.080 --> 01:49:14.120
Proper tracks. Right.

01:49:14.260 --> 01:49:19.580
Proper tracks. And also, like, there were certain stages when I was restarting the game

01:49:19.580 --> 01:49:22.760
or going back to a previous stage to just continue playing it.

01:49:23.020 --> 01:49:26.020
There are certain things that, like, it's just satisfying when you start up again

01:49:26.020 --> 01:49:28.300
on that stage and you hear the music and you're like,

01:49:28.420 --> 01:49:30.800
oh, I'm so glad I get to hear this track again.

01:49:30.960 --> 01:49:32.640
Yeah. Yeah, the stage kind of sucks.

01:49:33.600 --> 01:49:36.260
I hate, like, you know, how difficult this game is.

01:49:36.440 --> 01:49:38.100
But the music, like, welcomes you back in.

01:49:38.100 --> 01:49:39.100
It makes you feel good.

01:49:39.160 --> 01:49:40.780
It, like, fits the experience, too.

01:49:40.920 --> 01:49:43.580
It feels action-y and cool and awesome.

01:49:43.880 --> 01:49:45.020
And you're doing awesome things.

01:49:45.020 --> 01:49:46.680
It keeps your motivation relatively high.

01:49:46.800 --> 01:49:51.340
Yeah. Yeah, I'd say that the music in this one is just as good as, you know,

01:49:51.400 --> 01:49:55.740
like a Super Mario Brothers, A Legend of Zelda, Metroid, a...

01:49:55.740 --> 01:49:57.220
1942. Yeah.

01:49:57.660 --> 01:49:59.640
Mike Tyson's Punch-Out had great music.

01:49:59.780 --> 01:50:05.140
We liked that music. Like, I'd say this is probably my favorite soundtrack so far on the NES.

01:50:05.280 --> 01:50:06.120
Wow. Yeah.

01:50:06.280 --> 01:50:07.660
You are high on a lot of soundtracks.

01:50:07.660 --> 01:50:08.640
I loved Castlevania.

01:50:08.760 --> 01:50:11.320
Yeah. Everybody knows how I feel about Castlevania,

01:50:11.500 --> 01:50:13.520
but I think this music is just slightly better

01:50:13.520 --> 01:50:16.200
because you don't need to be Dracula to enjoy it.

01:50:16.880 --> 01:50:20.160
The Castlevania music is kind of dark and dark and dancey, sure.

01:50:20.380 --> 01:50:22.140
Like, it has, like, this dance vibe.

01:50:22.140 --> 01:50:23.260
No, it does have this dance vibe, too.

01:50:23.380 --> 01:50:25.060
No, I mean, yeah, it definitely has a dance vibe.

01:50:25.160 --> 01:50:26.340
I just don't know how dark it is.

01:50:26.440 --> 01:50:28.560
Well, I mean, like, you know, it's as dark as an NES game can get.

01:50:28.680 --> 01:50:30.480
I just mean, like, there's certain points where it's like,

01:50:30.560 --> 01:50:33.440
yeah, you probably want to play that music on, like, Halloween and not, like,

01:50:33.920 --> 01:50:35.320
I'm just walking to work.

01:50:35.580 --> 01:50:37.400
But it's like, Mega Man, if I'm walking to work,

01:50:37.500 --> 01:50:40.420
I might accidentally use my power...

01:50:40.420 --> 01:50:41.880
To throw scissors at someone.

01:50:41.880 --> 01:50:47.200
Yeah, my power cannon might accidentally turn on my scissor mode

01:50:48.340 --> 01:50:49.480
and cut somebody.

01:50:50.400 --> 01:50:51.400
Music was great, though.

01:50:51.680 --> 01:50:53.560
Gotta say that. To sum it up, we hate the music.

01:50:53.800 --> 01:50:59.560
Yes. And one final thought on development from Akira Kitamura.

01:50:59.700 --> 01:51:00.720
And I know a lot of people are going to be asking,

01:51:00.840 --> 01:51:03.020
why didn't you grab anything from Inafune?

01:51:03.440 --> 01:51:04.840
Well, it's not that I don't like...

01:51:04.840 --> 01:51:07.380
Inafune's an imposter and you should not like him.

01:51:07.480 --> 01:51:08.960
It's not that I feel that way.

01:51:08.960 --> 01:51:11.820
It's that there's so much information available on Mega Man, guys.

01:51:11.880 --> 01:51:13.780
Guys, there's so much information available

01:51:13.780 --> 01:51:16.400
that I can't cover it in one podcast.

01:51:16.640 --> 01:51:18.200
Like, we have to do...

01:51:18.200 --> 01:51:19.340
We have to start a new podcast.

01:51:19.500 --> 01:51:20.760
Right, on just Mega Man, right?

01:51:20.880 --> 01:51:23.340
You could. You could do a whole franchise of, like,

01:51:23.380 --> 01:51:24.740
here's the Mega Man comics

01:51:24.740 --> 01:51:26.660
and here's what the Mega Man animes did

01:51:26.660 --> 01:51:28.060
and here's what the Mega Man...

01:51:28.060 --> 01:51:29.200
Legends are. Right.

01:51:29.420 --> 01:51:30.940
Yeah, exactly. There's Mega Man Legends.

01:51:31.080 --> 01:51:31.920
There's Mega Man X.

01:51:32.040 --> 01:51:33.500
There's Mega Man Zero.

01:51:34.000 --> 01:51:35.960
Fan fiction. Mega Man soccer is a thing.

01:51:36.120 --> 01:51:37.340
Yes, I know you know that.

01:51:39.320 --> 01:51:40.180
I know you know that.

01:51:40.220 --> 01:51:41.700
A weird exchange that just happened.

01:51:41.880 --> 01:51:43.480
It's true. There's so much to talk about.

01:51:43.660 --> 01:51:44.960
So I just grabbed this interview

01:51:44.960 --> 01:51:46.500
because I found it to be, you know,

01:51:46.660 --> 01:51:48.080
a lot of interviews that we grab

01:51:48.080 --> 01:51:49.120
are usually terrible.

01:51:50.000 --> 01:51:53.120
They're like, well, when we made a game company,

01:51:53.120 --> 01:51:55.120
we decided that we wanted to make a game

01:51:55.120 --> 01:51:56.280
and so we made a game

01:51:56.280 --> 01:51:57.600
and we hoped that people enjoy it.

01:51:57.700 --> 01:51:59.300
You know, like, that's our usual interview.

01:51:59.300 --> 01:52:02.520
So I like what Kitamura's bringing to the table here.

01:52:02.740 --> 01:52:04.220
And so this was just him

01:52:04.220 --> 01:52:06.760
on the very beginning of Mega Man.

01:52:07.560 --> 01:52:09.200
After a period of study

01:52:09.200 --> 01:52:11.180
and working on various projects,

01:52:11.460 --> 01:52:13.720
the Mega Man development began at Capcom.

01:52:13.940 --> 01:52:16.360
But as I thought about game design then,

01:52:16.580 --> 01:52:18.340
I started to wonder if designers

01:52:18.340 --> 01:52:19.820
had really thought deeply

01:52:19.820 --> 01:52:22.120
about enemy placement and behavior.

01:52:22.120 --> 01:52:25.160
I decided to play a bunch of different games

01:52:25.160 --> 01:52:27.900
and study just those difficult sections,

01:52:28.580 --> 01:52:30.280
replaying them over and over.

01:52:30.620 --> 01:52:32.040
And it was through that

01:52:32.040 --> 01:52:33.140
that Kitamura learned

01:52:33.140 --> 01:52:36.320
about specifically placing enemies

01:52:36.320 --> 01:52:38.300
versus, you know, everything else.

01:52:38.540 --> 01:52:39.660
Spawning. Yeah, spawning enemies.

01:52:39.880 --> 01:52:42.520
And I think that there's a very deliberate approach

01:52:42.520 --> 01:52:43.700
to Mega Man that like,

01:52:43.820 --> 01:52:45.360
whether it's frustrating or not,

01:52:45.580 --> 01:52:46.880
it was played through by people

01:52:46.880 --> 01:52:48.580
who designed them a certain way.

01:52:48.580 --> 01:52:50.020
This is a guy who also calculated

01:52:50.020 --> 01:52:51.340
Mega Man's walking speed

01:52:51.340 --> 01:52:52.540
to figure out if you can beat

01:52:52.540 --> 01:52:53.760
the whole game in an hour.

01:52:54.740 --> 01:52:56.020
All these things kept in mind.

01:52:56.100 --> 01:52:59.280
I think it's a very polished game

01:52:59.280 --> 01:53:01.200
in the sense that the designers

01:53:01.200 --> 01:53:03.160
got what they wanted out of it.

01:53:03.260 --> 01:53:05.400
You know, given the limitations of the system

01:53:05.400 --> 01:53:07.280
and their programming knowledge at the time.

01:53:07.440 --> 01:53:09.740
They definitely felt like they delivered

01:53:10.480 --> 01:53:12.580
a product to the people.

01:53:12.980 --> 01:53:14.960
And whether we find that experience

01:53:14.960 --> 01:53:15.840
frustrating at times,

01:53:15.920 --> 01:53:16.940
which I certainly did,

01:53:17.440 --> 01:53:18.760
there's no doubt that like

01:53:18.760 --> 01:53:20.240
anything that was in this game,

01:53:20.440 --> 01:53:22.160
they meant to happen

01:53:22.160 --> 01:53:23.200
outside of like, you know,

01:53:24.340 --> 01:53:27.100
glitches and general like errors

01:53:27.100 --> 01:53:29.600
and stuff like that. You know, I think that for the most part,

01:53:30.100 --> 01:53:31.180
you might think like,

01:53:31.280 --> 01:53:32.840
you might disagree with an enemy's placement

01:53:32.840 --> 01:53:35.540
in this game, but it seems like they really tested that stuff

01:53:35.540 --> 01:53:37.680
and made sure that it was how they wanted it

01:53:37.680 --> 01:53:39.840
to spawn into programming.

01:53:39.980 --> 01:53:41.100
And it's funny because that seems like

01:53:41.100 --> 01:53:43.860
such a pillar of game design,

01:53:43.860 --> 01:53:45.280
but it's something that they're like

01:53:45.280 --> 01:53:47.820
just kind of almost pioneering.

01:53:48.000 --> 01:53:49.120
I mean, people have done this kind of thing

01:53:49.120 --> 01:53:50.520
before with enemy placement,

01:53:50.580 --> 01:53:52.460
but like so many people aren't doing that

01:53:52.460 --> 01:53:54.220
at this point. Either not thinking of it

01:53:54.220 --> 01:53:55.360
or not caring to do it,

01:53:55.520 --> 01:53:57.180
where it's like makes such a difference

01:53:57.180 --> 01:53:59.320
in this game. So it's just crazy to me that like,

01:53:59.460 --> 01:54:00.860
now that seems like a no-brainer,

01:54:01.040 --> 01:54:02.980
but that they were like thinking,

01:54:02.980 --> 01:54:05.880
we should plan where the enemy's.

01:54:06.000 --> 01:54:08.480
Yeah, that's true. It's actually like a breakthrough moment

01:54:08.480 --> 01:54:10.040
for a game kind of thing.

01:54:10.140 --> 01:54:11.380
And I appreciate that

01:54:11.380 --> 01:54:12.600
because if you think about

01:54:12.600 --> 01:54:16.560
even just the idea of video game programmers

01:54:16.560 --> 01:54:20.020
at the time playing other people's video games,

01:54:20.520 --> 01:54:22.140
like how often was that done too?

01:54:22.280 --> 01:54:24.580
Like how many people that programmed

01:54:24.580 --> 01:54:25.720
the Karate Kid game,

01:54:25.800 --> 01:54:26.920
we're picking on it today,

01:54:27.280 --> 01:54:28.880
how many people that programmed that game

01:54:28.880 --> 01:54:31.660
played other games around the time

01:54:31.660 --> 01:54:33.160
that Karate Kid came out and said,

01:54:33.600 --> 01:54:34.960
ours is a similar experience.

01:54:36.000 --> 01:54:37.640
Like who, how many of them did that?

01:54:37.740 --> 01:54:38.920
I don't know how many of them did that.

01:54:39.300 --> 01:54:40.480
That's a crap shoot

01:54:40.480 --> 01:54:43.500
because I feel like it's intellectually disingenuous

01:54:44.100 --> 01:54:46.460
to say that we think that these programmers

01:54:46.460 --> 01:54:48.120
did not play video games.

01:54:48.240 --> 01:54:49.040
Like they had at least,

01:54:49.160 --> 01:54:52.800
they had to at least know some basics.

01:54:53.140 --> 01:54:55.180
Sure. To be able to program.

01:54:55.340 --> 01:54:57.860
I agree. I'm positive they played video games.

01:54:58.100 --> 01:55:01.940
I'm saying. But as like a study.

01:55:02.220 --> 01:55:04.140
Yeah. A study is what I'm referring to.

01:55:04.220 --> 01:55:06.000
I don't know if they were actually thinking of it

01:55:06.000 --> 01:55:07.240
in a way of like,

01:55:08.220 --> 01:55:10.800
again, like an intellectual exchange of ideas.

01:55:12.100 --> 01:55:13.760
Gleaning concepts from other games

01:55:13.760 --> 01:55:14.560
and saying like,

01:55:14.700 --> 01:55:17.700
how can I morph this into something fun and interesting

01:55:18.540 --> 01:55:19.580
and new? Right.

01:55:19.700 --> 01:55:21.160
Breaking it down. What about this works

01:55:21.160 --> 01:55:23.380
and how can we incorporate it into our game?

01:55:23.580 --> 01:55:24.960
So yeah, I think that that is,

01:55:25.100 --> 01:55:29.560
that is a good question. I don't know if a lot of the games that we played

01:55:29.560 --> 01:55:31.380
have gone through that,

01:55:32.480 --> 01:55:33.520
that process.

01:55:33.720 --> 01:55:35.220
Right. Any stray thoughts?

01:55:35.900 --> 01:55:37.120
Oh yeah, I got a stray thought.

01:55:37.420 --> 01:55:41.600
One thing that I've noticed about a lot of these old games

01:55:41.600 --> 01:55:46.240
is that there isn't a lot of meta game.

01:55:46.360 --> 01:55:48.640
And I don't mean that in the actual way

01:55:48.640 --> 01:55:50.940
that that term is usually used.

01:55:51.200 --> 01:55:53.560
It's only because I can't really think of the actual,

01:55:53.560 --> 01:55:54.860
like framing devices,

01:55:55.040 --> 01:55:56.260
framing devices in a game

01:55:58.660 --> 01:56:00.340
where most of these games,

01:56:00.500 --> 01:56:01.780
you got like the start screen,

01:56:01.840 --> 01:56:03.920
you just start and it starts the game

01:56:03.920 --> 01:56:06.240
with maybe a little like quick video.

01:56:07.400 --> 01:56:11.400
So the idea of like a designed level select

01:56:12.540 --> 01:56:14.740
and then a designed menu

01:56:14.740 --> 01:56:18.200
that isn't just like so bare bones.

01:56:19.180 --> 01:56:21.980
Like there's actual like graphic design

01:56:21.980 --> 01:56:23.020
put into this game

01:56:23.020 --> 01:56:25.680
to sort of like guide your,

01:56:25.800 --> 01:56:27.200
guide your experience.

01:56:27.500 --> 01:56:28.380
Do you know what I mean?

01:56:28.580 --> 01:56:30.480
I don't know. I think I'm being a little too.

01:56:31.700 --> 01:56:33.540
No, I understand what you're saying.

01:56:33.940 --> 01:56:36.540
And like also when you select a level,

01:56:36.800 --> 01:56:37.900
like the boss appears.

01:56:38.220 --> 01:56:39.120
Yeah. Like this is the guy.

01:56:39.620 --> 01:56:41.100
You can see the presentation.

01:56:41.400 --> 01:56:42.860
I don't know why I couldn't just say that.

01:56:43.000 --> 01:56:44.860
It's like stylized in a very specific way.

01:56:45.120 --> 01:56:46.940
Stylization of presentation and graphic design.

01:56:47.260 --> 01:56:49.840
You can sort of see it come out of the primordial ooze

01:56:49.840 --> 01:56:54.480
of all this really janky utilitarian design.

01:56:55.500 --> 01:56:56.620
Like we're just going from,

01:56:56.840 --> 01:56:57.960
here's the main menu screen

01:56:57.960 --> 01:56:58.920
and then here's the game.

01:56:59.100 --> 01:57:01.920
Now there's actual like elements of style coming into

01:57:01.920 --> 01:57:03.640
how the way it's presenting information.

01:57:04.020 --> 01:57:06.880
And I'm just glad to finally see some of that stuff.

01:57:07.080 --> 01:57:09.080
Yeah. It's funny because it's done so well

01:57:09.080 --> 01:57:12.060
that like I almost, it's almost in the back of my mind.

01:57:12.120 --> 01:57:13.820
Like I noticed it like subconsciously.

01:57:14.100 --> 01:57:16.300
It adds to the experience, but I never really thought about that.

01:57:16.540 --> 01:57:18.780
Because in the beginning when I said that like,

01:57:18.940 --> 01:57:21.820
oh, I can see that like this still feels like Mega Man,

01:57:22.400 --> 01:57:23.480
well like Mega Man 8,

01:57:23.740 --> 01:57:25.280
like in 97 or 96,

01:57:25.560 --> 01:57:27.640
still felt very similar to Mega Man,

01:57:27.640 --> 01:57:29.720
one. And it's a lot,

01:57:29.820 --> 01:57:31.680
it has a lot to do because of like the,

01:57:32.240 --> 01:57:34.600
just the stylish elements that they have.

01:57:34.700 --> 01:57:36.720
Like whether he's just warping into the stage

01:57:36.720 --> 01:57:39.840
or the way that they announce,

01:57:40.980 --> 01:57:42.820
announce the bosses before the level.

01:57:42.980 --> 01:57:44.660
Like all of those stylistic elements

01:57:44.660 --> 01:57:46.900
are very like basic,

01:57:47.340 --> 01:57:49.720
but I think it adds to the experience a lot.

01:57:49.840 --> 01:57:51.180
Yeah. Like warping into the stage,

01:57:51.280 --> 01:57:53.960
like you said, that's, we never started a stage

01:57:53.960 --> 01:57:56.120
without just automatically being there before.

01:57:56.580 --> 01:57:58.780
It could have been just a big black screen

01:57:58.780 --> 01:58:00.820
with games A through H on it.

01:58:00.940 --> 01:58:02.540
Yeah. You know? Yeah. Right.

01:58:02.700 --> 01:58:04.960
And there's still like a way to go.

01:58:05.100 --> 01:58:06.600
And the worst version of this

01:58:06.600 --> 01:58:08.100
is that you hit start

01:58:08.100 --> 01:58:09.640
and it just automatically starts you

01:58:09.640 --> 01:58:10.460
and cut main stage

01:58:10.460 --> 01:58:11.880
and you have no decision over anything.

01:58:12.020 --> 01:58:13.680
Yeah. It's like, but there's also like,

01:58:14.240 --> 01:58:16.680
there's still, there's still things to stride,

01:58:16.760 --> 01:58:18.200
you know, there's still a lot of strides to take

01:58:18.200 --> 01:58:21.500
to get to the final like polish of presentation

01:58:21.500 --> 01:58:23.180
because we don't know

01:58:23.180 --> 01:58:24.860
who that girl is at the end.

01:58:24.960 --> 01:58:28.260
Yeah. And we don't know like what exactly,

01:58:28.540 --> 01:58:29.800
like if you didn't read the back of the box,

01:58:29.860 --> 01:58:31.420
we don't know like what the stakes are

01:58:31.420 --> 01:58:33.780
and stuff like that. A nice little like text box

01:58:33.780 --> 01:58:35.120
or something would have gone a long way.

01:58:35.200 --> 01:58:36.540
Yeah. But they chose not to include that stuff.

01:58:36.860 --> 01:58:37.940
Yeah, you're right. I mean, that's,

01:58:38.040 --> 01:58:39.020
I guess why I mean like

01:58:39.020 --> 01:58:41.160
this is the primordial version of that.

01:58:41.160 --> 01:58:42.340
You're totally correct on that.

01:58:42.480 --> 01:58:43.500
And I just want to say too,

01:58:43.760 --> 01:58:44.880
my own straight thought,

01:58:45.760 --> 01:58:46.960
you know, we were talking about how like

01:58:46.960 --> 01:58:48.760
they tested this game

01:58:48.760 --> 01:58:49.600
and they shipped a product

01:58:49.600 --> 01:58:51.540
that they were proud of. And I definitely think that's true,

01:58:51.540 --> 01:58:53.120
but I don't want to like frame it

01:58:53.120 --> 01:58:56.020
as that like the Mega Man development team

01:58:56.020 --> 01:58:58.040
thought that like Mega Man 1 was perfect.

01:58:58.500 --> 01:59:01.520
They actually didn't like a lot of their Mega Man,

01:59:01.640 --> 01:59:02.500
early Mega Man games

01:59:02.500 --> 01:59:04.880
because the development time was so,

01:59:05.280 --> 01:59:06.300
was so small.

01:59:07.100 --> 01:59:08.800
Forget about how much time Mega Man 1 had.

01:59:08.900 --> 01:59:11.040
Mega Man 2 only had like confirmed.

01:59:11.160 --> 01:59:12.020
Three months.

01:59:12.400 --> 01:59:13.580
Wow. To make that game.

01:59:14.060 --> 01:59:15.580
So like, they definitely felt,

01:59:16.120 --> 01:59:18.300
you know, like Mega Man 1 had its weaknesses

01:59:18.300 --> 01:59:19.920
and that there was things that like

01:59:19.920 --> 01:59:21.240
they could have polished better.

01:59:21.500 --> 01:59:22.660
But I do think it's nice

01:59:22.660 --> 01:59:24.140
that they even just thought about

01:59:24.140 --> 01:59:25.380
how they would place enemies

01:59:25.380 --> 01:59:26.420
and test them out like that.

01:59:26.480 --> 01:59:27.620
So that's really more what I meant.

01:59:27.700 --> 01:59:28.780
I didn't mean that they thought

01:59:28.780 --> 01:59:30.140
that they shipped the perfect game.

01:59:30.300 --> 01:59:31.660
No, I know what you mean.

01:59:31.820 --> 01:59:33.500
Yeah. But it does explain too

01:59:33.500 --> 01:59:35.040
why the graphic designer

01:59:35.040 --> 01:59:36.200
only had one day to make.

01:59:36.200 --> 01:59:37.640
Yeah, exactly. I mean,

01:59:37.760 --> 01:59:39.220
Kitamore even goes on to explain

01:59:39.220 --> 01:59:40.520
like his biggest problem

01:59:40.520 --> 01:59:41.360
with Mega Man 1

01:59:41.360 --> 01:59:43.060
in this interview. He says,

01:59:43.060 --> 01:59:44.360
the truth is there were

01:59:44.360 --> 01:59:45.200
a number of flaws

01:59:45.200 --> 01:59:46.580
in Mega Man 1.

01:59:46.800 --> 01:59:48.680
The way we thought about weapons

01:59:48.680 --> 01:59:50.140
and the Robot Masters then

01:59:50.140 --> 01:59:51.400
was different than how we thought

01:59:51.400 --> 01:59:52.580
about them in later games.

01:59:52.900 --> 01:59:53.880
In the first game,

01:59:54.020 --> 01:59:55.220
we designed the weapons

01:59:55.220 --> 01:59:56.860
by thinking about the corresponding

01:59:56.860 --> 01:59:58.340
Robot Master weakness.

01:59:58.740 --> 02:00:00.640
This focus on the Robot Masters

02:00:00.640 --> 02:00:01.540
sometimes caused us

02:00:01.540 --> 02:00:02.600
to neglect thinking about

02:00:02.600 --> 02:00:04.020
whether the weapons were fun

02:00:04.020 --> 02:00:05.200
or how they would actually

02:00:05.200 --> 02:00:06.020
work in the game

02:00:06.020 --> 02:00:07.400
or how they could help solve puzzles.

02:00:07.840 --> 02:00:08.780
So I think that is interesting

02:00:09.200 --> 02:00:10.140
that, you know,

02:00:10.240 --> 02:00:11.240
it's strictly designed

02:00:11.240 --> 02:00:12.500
from a combat perspective,

02:00:12.900 --> 02:00:14.300
but we found them to work,

02:00:14.420 --> 02:00:15.560
I found them to work pretty well

02:00:15.560 --> 02:00:16.940
as solving stage problems.

02:00:17.240 --> 02:00:18.380
I think so too. I wouldn't,

02:00:18.420 --> 02:00:19.840
I'd say he's being a little too hard on himself.

02:00:20.620 --> 02:00:21.680
I think that might be

02:00:21.680 --> 02:00:22.520
one of the first times

02:00:22.520 --> 02:00:25.580
I would agree with like a graphic,

02:00:25.740 --> 02:00:27.420
not a graphic designer, a video game designer

02:00:27.420 --> 02:00:28.520
being too hard on themselves.

02:00:29.800 --> 02:00:32.280
Because yeah, they did have that whole like,

02:00:32.420 --> 02:00:33.280
you know, one of the,

02:00:33.700 --> 02:00:35.060
one of the philosophies

02:00:35.060 --> 02:00:35.880
of game design is like

02:00:35.880 --> 02:00:37.440
if you're going to create a,

02:00:38.860 --> 02:00:40.240
you're going to create a mechanic,

02:00:40.460 --> 02:00:42.000
better have multiple uses

02:00:42.000 --> 02:00:43.480
or it's going to fall flat.

02:00:43.640 --> 02:00:45.740
And they just sort of did that by accident,

02:00:45.760 --> 02:00:46.680
it sounds like. Yeah.

02:00:46.680 --> 02:00:48.160
Because he doesn't think he did it well enough.

02:00:48.440 --> 02:00:50.720
Right, right. And I do like that though,

02:00:50.740 --> 02:00:51.680
he does go on to say

02:00:51.680 --> 02:00:52.960
that in future Mega Man games

02:00:52.960 --> 02:00:55.380
they will always plan the power-ups first

02:00:55.380 --> 02:00:56.420
and then think about

02:00:56.420 --> 02:00:57.520
what Robot Master

02:00:57.520 --> 02:00:59.160
could like use them.

02:00:59.320 --> 02:01:00.360
That seems how

02:01:00.360 --> 02:01:01.700
game design would work.

02:01:01.860 --> 02:01:02.980
Right, it seems like

02:01:02.980 --> 02:01:03.860
at the time

02:01:03.860 --> 02:01:04.820
it's a very Nintendo

02:01:04.820 --> 02:01:06.240
way of thinking.

02:01:06.540 --> 02:01:07.580
Yeah, you do the action first

02:01:07.580 --> 02:01:08.380
and then you figure out

02:01:08.380 --> 02:01:10.380
how it's created around that.

02:01:10.400 --> 02:01:11.660
Yeah, they even have gone on record

02:01:11.660 --> 02:01:12.720
saying that they still do that

02:01:12.720 --> 02:01:13.620
where they come up

02:01:13.620 --> 02:01:14.980
with the mechanic first

02:01:14.980 --> 02:01:17.300
and then think about which of their Nintendo franchises

02:01:17.300 --> 02:01:18.180
it would be good for.

02:01:18.400 --> 02:01:20.300
Right. You know, so I appreciate

02:01:20.300 --> 02:01:22.260
that kind of mindset

02:01:22.260 --> 02:01:23.300
in game making

02:01:23.300 --> 02:01:24.220
where you think about like

02:01:24.220 --> 02:01:25.480
what is the gameplay element

02:01:25.480 --> 02:01:26.560
first before you think about

02:01:26.560 --> 02:01:28.180
because otherwise you do wind up with

02:01:28.180 --> 02:01:29.240
like you said,

02:01:29.760 --> 02:01:30.820
you know, with the Mega Man games

02:01:30.820 --> 02:01:32.220
that there's so many of them

02:01:32.220 --> 02:01:33.520
but a lot of them are just like

02:01:34.640 --> 02:01:35.540
either the same

02:01:35.540 --> 02:01:36.420
or there's just

02:01:36.420 --> 02:01:37.520
not enough different

02:01:37.520 --> 02:01:39.320
about them. It just gets into this thing

02:01:39.320 --> 02:01:40.580
where it's like they all just kind of like

02:01:40.580 --> 02:01:41.740
meld after a while.

02:01:41.840 --> 02:01:43.340
This is the very first Mega Man game

02:01:43.340 --> 02:01:44.640
so it feels super unique

02:01:44.640 --> 02:01:46.080
but how will we feel

02:01:46.080 --> 02:01:47.400
when we get to like Mega Man 5?

02:01:47.620 --> 02:01:49.020
Right. Yeah. I wonder.

02:01:50.180 --> 02:01:51.700
I have another stray thought

02:01:51.700 --> 02:01:52.960
but it's really,

02:01:53.120 --> 02:01:54.120
really small. Oh, I love it.

02:01:55.080 --> 02:01:56.660
I love the turnaround sound.

02:01:57.460 --> 02:01:59.140
You know, I don't know it.

02:02:00.100 --> 02:02:01.140
Oh, yes,

02:02:01.900 --> 02:02:02.760
yes. I like that

02:02:02.760 --> 02:02:03.760
that's what triggered your memory.

02:02:04.320 --> 02:02:05.360
It's true, it's true.

02:02:05.600 --> 02:02:06.460
I don't even know this.

02:02:07.200 --> 02:02:08.320
The turnaround sound

02:02:08.320 --> 02:02:10.300
when you like skid and you switch directions

02:02:10.300 --> 02:02:12.260
he makes this very

02:02:12.260 --> 02:02:13.620
like signature sound.

02:02:13.840 --> 02:02:15.000
It's funny that you mention that though too

02:02:15.000 --> 02:02:16.440
because that's something that only I think

02:02:16.440 --> 02:02:18.060
Mega Man 1 has

02:02:18.060 --> 02:02:18.940
maybe Mega Man 2

02:02:18.940 --> 02:02:21.160
has it as well is that that skid

02:02:21.700 --> 02:02:23.140
in future Mega Man games

02:02:23.140 --> 02:02:24.660
they will actually stop that

02:02:24.660 --> 02:02:26.700
and make him like stop on a dime.

02:02:26.860 --> 02:02:27.980
The skid I'm not crazy about

02:02:27.980 --> 02:02:29.520
but the sound is great.

02:02:29.720 --> 02:02:30.900
Of all the things that were similar

02:02:30.900 --> 02:02:32.300
between this game

02:02:32.300 --> 02:02:33.300
and future Mega Man

02:02:33.300 --> 02:02:34.140
that was one thing

02:02:34.140 --> 02:02:35.100
I did not remember

02:02:35.100 --> 02:02:36.360
was how he was sort of like

02:02:36.360 --> 02:02:37.480
a little slippery.

02:02:37.660 --> 02:02:38.800
But it adds some weight to him,

02:02:38.820 --> 02:02:40.020
right? Don't we like that in Mario?

02:02:40.280 --> 02:02:41.780
I guess like yes it doesn't work

02:02:41.780 --> 02:02:43.520
as well here because the platforms

02:02:43.520 --> 02:02:44.860
are tiny.

02:02:45.380 --> 02:02:47.020
Yeah. If you were constantly

02:02:47.020 --> 02:02:48.500
just jumping on like

02:02:49.140 --> 02:02:51.180
small like coin blocks

02:02:51.180 --> 02:02:52.560
in Mario as you went

02:02:52.560 --> 02:02:53.660
through the stage that would get

02:02:53.660 --> 02:02:54.700
frustrating too I guess.

02:02:54.920 --> 02:02:59.600
Yeah. It will not arriv arriv

02:02:59.600 --> 02:03:04.840
will arriv will Thank you.

02:04:00.860 --> 02:04:03.440
Mega Man 2 through 6 will all come out on the NES.

02:04:04.580 --> 02:04:05.960
Almost like one a year.

02:04:06.200 --> 02:04:07.260
We have one next year.

02:04:07.420 --> 02:04:08.380
We have one the year after that.

02:04:08.520 --> 02:04:12.180
I don't know when they skip, but I think they do miss like a year or two here and there.

02:04:12.400 --> 02:04:16.440
But for the most part, it's like every single year a Mega Man game comes out.

02:04:16.560 --> 02:04:17.580
So we have that to look forward to.

02:04:17.660 --> 02:04:21.040
Mega Man will continue to be in our lives more often than Mario.

02:04:21.500 --> 02:04:22.520
You know, isn't that kind of weird?

02:04:22.520 --> 02:04:23.760
Yeah, yeah. For an NES thing.

02:04:25.180 --> 02:04:27.800
The Mega Man games now go all the way up to 11.

02:04:28.720 --> 02:04:30.620
That's just for the regular series, though.

02:04:30.780 --> 02:04:31.800
Yeah, yeah. For the regular series.

02:04:32.020 --> 02:04:34.280
So the core series as it's referred to.

02:04:34.760 --> 02:04:37.040
So Mega Man 7 came out on the Super Nintendo.

02:04:37.660 --> 02:04:40.780
Mega Man 8 came out on the PlayStation 1.

02:04:41.360 --> 02:04:42.220
And the Saturn.

02:04:42.220 --> 02:04:43.420
Yeah, and the Saturn.

02:04:43.600 --> 02:04:49.060
9 and 10 came out on the Wii as downloadable games.

02:04:49.280 --> 02:04:54.540
And then I think somehow eventually they got re-released as fake cartridges.

02:04:54.540 --> 02:04:58.200
I don't know if they actually work on your NES or not, but people love them.

02:04:58.340 --> 02:05:03.560
And they actually are. 9 and 10, just for what it's worth, they're not as good looking as, say, 8 was.

02:05:03.600 --> 02:05:07.000
They actually went back to the roots of what 1 through 6 looked like.

02:05:07.000 --> 02:05:11.400
I think 9 also did a shout out to the original Terrible Box, too.

02:05:11.400 --> 02:05:15.500
I think it did in its design or something like that.

02:05:15.560 --> 02:05:17.300
I do remember that they released a physical version.

02:05:17.960 --> 02:05:24.560
And then after 9 and 10, 11 just came out recently and added like a...

02:05:24.560 --> 02:05:32.480
It's a mixed reviews. Yeah, it adds like a slowdown ability that you have and also like a constant power thing you have.

02:05:32.600 --> 02:05:36.280
It's really hard to explain here, but like, you know, it's different.

02:05:36.400 --> 02:05:37.800
Loot boxes? Yeah.

02:05:38.180 --> 02:05:46.980
I don't know. It doesn't have loot boxes, but on top of Mega Man 2 through 6 on NES, we also get six Mega Man games on the Game Boy.

02:05:47.560 --> 02:05:50.520
Oh, wow. Six different versions of Mega Man on the Game Boy.

02:05:51.000 --> 02:05:55.400
And they're only slightly different from their NES counterparts, but I imagine they're...

02:05:55.400 --> 02:05:57.760
Are they meant to be like counter to...

02:05:57.760 --> 02:05:58.760
No, no, no.

02:05:59.120 --> 02:06:00.680
They're their own. It's like its own thing.

02:06:01.180 --> 02:06:04.340
Similar to like how Super Mario Land is its own thing on Game Boy.

02:06:04.480 --> 02:06:07.180
It's not like Super Mario Land is Super Mario Brothers.

02:06:07.540 --> 02:06:09.620
Right. It's a different, separate game.

02:06:09.840 --> 02:06:13.380
Yeah, it's its own thing. And then, of course, you have the Mega Man X games.

02:06:14.020 --> 02:06:15.260
The X games.

02:06:15.800 --> 02:06:16.900
I said that earlier.

02:06:16.980 --> 02:06:19.360
The X series of games that...

02:06:19.360 --> 02:06:24.420
Whatever. The reason why I bring them up and not all the other franchises that Mega Man has is because they're the most similar...

02:06:24.420 --> 02:06:26.920
Yes. ...to the Mega Man core franchise.

02:06:27.280 --> 02:06:33.000
And so you have X, which starts on the SNES, and then it goes all the way up to X8 on the PS2.

02:06:33.160 --> 02:06:35.160
And that also has now a collection of games.

02:06:36.200 --> 02:06:38.400
But, you know, that one kind of gets weird too.

02:06:38.540 --> 02:06:42.260
Like X1, X2, and X3, I think, are like pretty well regarded.

02:06:42.260 --> 02:06:44.480
But I think X4 through 8 are considered a mess.

02:06:45.020 --> 02:06:46.320
So, I don't know.

02:06:46.560 --> 02:06:48.560
Let me know, Mega Man fans, how those games are.

02:06:48.740 --> 02:06:50.040
I'm sure I'll get to them eventually.

02:06:50.360 --> 02:06:54.080
But I have not played anything other than X1, and I really like it.

02:06:54.220 --> 02:07:01.380
Yeah. I think I played X4, but I must have passed out after because I don't remember anything about it.

02:07:01.620 --> 02:07:04.740
Yeah. There are so many other games in the Mega Man franchise.

02:07:04.740 --> 02:07:08.120
But let's just talk about Mega Man 1's remakes.

02:07:08.500 --> 02:07:11.380
Because there are two that I know of.

02:07:11.380 --> 02:07:15.660
The one I mentioned. There's the one you mentioned, Mega Man Powered Up on PSP.

02:07:15.960 --> 02:07:22.640
It takes a chibi look to all of the Mega Man characters and everything, which is very strange.

02:07:23.080 --> 02:07:29.660
It also adds Oil Man and Time Man as the additional two bosses.

02:07:29.940 --> 02:07:31.440
Time? Time Man.

02:07:31.620 --> 02:07:34.240
I feel like that's so much more powerful than any of the other...

02:07:34.240 --> 02:07:37.020
Well, I didn't play this one because I don't own a PSP.

02:07:37.020 --> 02:07:47.100
But apparently, on Polygon, there's an ongoing series of where they rank the franchises of games in order from worst to best.

02:07:47.180 --> 02:07:49.440
And it's usually done by Jeremy Parrish.

02:07:49.500 --> 02:07:53.660
He says that Mega Man Powered Up is the best of the entire core franchise.

02:07:53.860 --> 02:07:54.780
Interesting. Yes.

02:07:55.040 --> 02:08:04.920
So I think if that's anything to check out, he says that it's pretty much just Mega Man 1, but everything about it is better and more refined and a true Mega Man experience.

02:08:04.920 --> 02:08:12.480
And then outside of Mega Man Powered Up, even earlier, Mega Man Wily Wars came out on the Sega Genesis.

02:08:12.780 --> 02:08:16.240
It was the first time that Mega Man was not on a Nintendo console.

02:08:16.660 --> 02:08:18.820
And the Genesis version...

02:08:18.820 --> 02:08:20.560
The Genesis, not version.

02:08:20.740 --> 02:08:23.800
The Genesis game is just the first three games.

02:08:23.900 --> 02:08:25.060
Mega Man 1, 2, and 3.

02:08:25.240 --> 02:08:31.560
But playable on the Genesis with 16-bit graphics, a new arranged soundtrack, and other things like that.

02:08:31.560 --> 02:08:36.000
So it's basically just like a plussed-up version, but I gotta say, I played through that one.

02:08:36.400 --> 02:08:37.800
Not that great. No.

02:08:38.420 --> 02:08:45.220
I feel like what they added doesn't really add much more, similar to how Powered Up they were saying,

02:08:45.320 --> 02:08:48.220
like, oh, all these added things make the game so much stronger than Mega Man 1.

02:08:48.420 --> 02:08:51.320
This one, it's like the arranged soundtrack, take it or leave it for me.

02:08:51.540 --> 02:08:54.400
The 16-bit graphics on the Genesis, they look kind of strange.

02:08:54.400 --> 02:09:01.340
And there's, like, there's, like, this weird introduction of, like, slowdown that I don't like versus the slowdown on the NES.

02:09:02.400 --> 02:09:12.020
So overall, I'd say, like, it's interesting to play through them on a Genesis, but I didn't find that they added a whole lot to the Mega Man 1 experience.

02:09:12.260 --> 02:09:13.800
Not necessary, I think.

02:09:13.800 --> 02:09:23.980
But if you're looking to play through more Mega Man games, I think, like, it's really interesting that they put the first three as, like, a remastered collection on the Genesis.

02:09:23.980 --> 02:09:31.080
And then on top of that, after you beat all three of those games, you unlock a new, like, exclusive mode called Wily Tower,

02:09:31.080 --> 02:09:36.100
where you go through and fight some new enemies and new Wily bosses and stuff like that.

02:09:36.180 --> 02:09:40.680
At least it's not as milked as some games with their remakes and remasters.

02:09:40.680 --> 02:09:41.580
That's true, yeah.

02:09:41.700 --> 02:09:42.880
I mean, sometimes we look at games.

02:09:43.020 --> 02:09:50.420
And then other times you have, you know, like, with Castlevania, Castlevania 1 and that kind of game system, like, you don't get that.

02:09:50.520 --> 02:09:52.820
That franchise doesn't make games, like, in that style anymore.

02:09:53.040 --> 02:10:03.500
No. Castlevania 1, 2, and 3, and Super Castlevania 4, like, those games are their own line of the Castlevania franchise, and they're done.

02:10:03.800 --> 02:10:04.640
Super Mario Bros.

02:10:04.720 --> 02:10:08.300
is still getting, like, Super Mario Maker or New Super Mario Bros.

02:10:08.440 --> 02:10:09.620
Like, those things are still happening.

02:10:09.820 --> 02:10:13.940
Yeah. While they still make other versions, Mega Man is still getting, like, who knows?

02:10:14.100 --> 02:10:16.360
I don't know how Mega Man 11 did, but there might be a 12.

02:10:16.640 --> 02:10:18.260
Like, they're still getting versions of their games.

02:10:18.400 --> 02:10:20.440
Some franchises don't continue to get that love.

02:10:20.600 --> 02:10:25.980
No. Yeah. Speaking of love, let's find out if we love this game in the Essential Games list.

02:10:28.840 --> 02:10:32.860
Okay, we've been talking about this game way too long, but I like it.

02:10:32.920 --> 02:10:34.100
I like it when the episodes go long.

02:10:34.460 --> 02:10:36.820
Sam, let us know your definitive answer.

02:10:37.160 --> 02:10:38.720
Is Mega Man Essential?

02:10:38.720 --> 02:10:41.100
For me, I think that this game is essential.

02:10:41.340 --> 02:10:44.520
I think that it's got, you know, combat.

02:10:44.800 --> 02:10:54.260
It's got platforming. It has a very free way of approaching it, and you can kind of, you have to think about how you want to do it.

02:10:54.400 --> 02:11:04.260
It's hard. It's got that Nintendo hard that, you know, some people find nostalgic, and some people are looking for when they want to play an NES game, and I think that this has it.

02:11:05.660 --> 02:11:13.340
I don't know if it will stay essential for me when we play more games like this, or when, you know, we play Mega Man 2.

02:11:15.080 --> 02:11:18.180
Like, if there's one to play, I don't know yet, because I've only played this one.

02:11:18.320 --> 02:11:20.360
But for right now, I'm going to say it's essential.

02:11:21.120 --> 02:11:22.340
All right. Joe?

02:11:23.340 --> 02:11:24.660
So, I went into the game.

02:11:24.660 --> 02:11:30.860
I actually did have, like, a preconceived notion of things that I heard about this game being, like, not the best Mega Man game.

02:11:31.320 --> 02:11:38.020
It being the one that, like, doesn't deliver on what Mega Man is, doesn't really get you, you know, it's not, like, you shouldn't play this one first, I've heard.

02:11:38.680 --> 02:11:45.960
And so I kind of went into this with a little bit of a negative thought, you know, idea about what the game was going to be, and it really kind of turned my opinion around.

02:11:46.500 --> 02:11:58.380
Just to run through, I just got a little list of the things that I liked about this game, which is that each level, as challenging and, like, Nintendo hard as they were, rarely felt unfair.

02:11:58.580 --> 02:12:03.280
Sometimes it did feel unfair, but most of the time it felt fair and, like, doable and achievable.

02:12:03.280 --> 02:12:07.060
The power-ups were satisfying to collect and use.

02:12:07.220 --> 02:12:12.260
The controls were better than most of the games that we've played on the NES.

02:12:12.860 --> 02:12:21.540
I mean, everything that we've been talking about through this game is so much head and shoulders above, like, 90% of the games we've played so far.

02:12:22.060 --> 02:12:29.440
The only thing about it that I, at one point, was grappling with whether or not it bothered me was when I got to the Dr.

02:12:29.540 --> 02:12:32.140
Wily stage, which I didn't really talk about too much how.

02:12:32.140 --> 02:12:43.040
So I was, at first, a little bothered by this stage being so long and so difficult, and when you die, you have to go back to the beginning of the stage, even though it's kind of like five stages in one.

02:12:43.400 --> 02:12:45.780
Made it almost seem, like, a little unfair at first.

02:12:46.040 --> 02:12:54.640
Until I kind of decided to approach the final level as, like, something else where it's, even if you don't beat it, it's something to keep the challenge going.

02:12:54.680 --> 02:12:56.820
It's not going to be the same as all the other levels.

02:12:56.820 --> 02:13:00.220
Is that that's, like, the thing you keep coming back to, trying to beat?

02:13:00.260 --> 02:13:02.840
That's where the challenge really, that's when you're in the big leagues.

02:13:03.160 --> 02:13:11.400
And even if you can't beat the game, I think it's definitely worth playing and worth getting to this final level and trying to beat it.

02:13:11.500 --> 02:13:14.860
But either way, whether you beat it or not, I think this is an essential game.

02:13:14.860 --> 02:13:19.420
So, according to our current rules, that means that it's essential, as of right now.

02:13:20.160 --> 02:13:21.280
I think that rule's going to change.

02:13:21.440 --> 02:13:24.420
But, Sean, you know, now you're free.

02:13:24.500 --> 02:13:26.280
You don't have to vote essential if you don't want to.

02:13:26.300 --> 02:13:27.940
You're free to pick whatever you want, you know?

02:13:28.160 --> 02:13:29.820
You can let the record state on your side.

02:13:29.940 --> 02:13:34.180
I had a lot to say, but now that it doesn't matter, I'm just going to abstain.

02:13:34.180 --> 02:13:38.660
But, no, I'm also going to jump on this bandwagon.

02:13:39.020 --> 02:13:47.360
I think that aside from the Nintendo royalty games, like the Mario, the Metroid, the Zelda,

02:13:48.800 --> 02:13:59.620
this game very quickly and with very little buildup established what a Mega Man game is.

02:13:59.620 --> 02:14:04.980
I know that, like, right now in 1987, like, yeah, Mega Man is Mega Man because that's the game that we're playing.

02:14:05.140 --> 02:14:11.800
But decades later, this is still what a Mega Man game is with a couple extra layers of paint on it.

02:14:12.760 --> 02:14:17.660
It established a bunch of interesting ways to structure a game.

02:14:18.220 --> 02:14:21.620
And maybe that's wrong, but this is the first time I'm seeing it chronologically.

02:14:22.660 --> 02:14:27.640
So, I'm going to attribute this, like, choose-your-own thing to Mega Man.

02:14:29.120 --> 02:14:36.460
And while it is frustratingly difficult for me, I still did have fun with it.

02:14:36.740 --> 02:14:48.640
And, like, games like this can really only work if, like, your frustration is balanced with feelings of accomplishment

02:14:48.640 --> 02:14:51.400
and just, it just feels fun to play.

02:14:51.400 --> 02:14:57.100
So, I do have, there are a lot of, like, blemishes on this game and it's not perfect,

02:14:57.120 --> 02:15:04.700
but I think that in the context of the history of side-scrolling shooters, side-scrolling platformer shooters,

02:15:04.880 --> 02:15:05.820
this is an essential game.

02:15:06.000 --> 02:15:08.160
All right. Pressure's on me.

02:15:08.680 --> 02:15:10.460
Better vote this one essential, I guess.

02:15:10.580 --> 02:15:12.940
No, or do I do a Metroid on you boys?

02:15:13.740 --> 02:15:17.080
Because a lot of people want me to live that down, that I voted Metroid not essential.

02:15:17.760 --> 02:15:19.600
And then there's other people who thank me for that.

02:15:19.880 --> 02:15:21.660
I get a lot of handshakes when I was walking down the street.

02:15:21.660 --> 02:15:22.840
You get recognized on the street.

02:15:23.200 --> 02:15:26.220
Yeah, yeah. A lot of people are just like, here's that guy who stood up to Metroid.

02:15:26.460 --> 02:15:28.020
And I'm like, I hate Samus.

02:15:28.820 --> 02:15:30.280
I'm part of the space pirates.

02:15:30.760 --> 02:15:33.860
No, for real, though, here's the weird thing.

02:15:34.620 --> 02:15:39.260
It's unfortunate that my vote for whether Mega Man is essential has to come with some caveats,

02:15:39.320 --> 02:15:46.440
but this vote for me reminds me so much of, like, when we voted Excitebike in Episode 5 Essential.

02:15:47.360 --> 02:15:48.900
In a very strange way.

02:15:49.020 --> 02:15:49.980
Here's the thing, okay?

02:15:49.980 --> 02:15:51.480
I played Mega Man 2.

02:15:51.580 --> 02:15:53.220
I remember it. It was fantastic.

02:15:54.300 --> 02:15:58.620
We haven't gotten up to that yet in nostalgia, and I don't know, you know, how I should.

02:15:58.720 --> 02:16:01.320
But I don't think that there has to be just one version of a game.

02:16:01.320 --> 02:16:03.900
I'd be shocked to find out I'm going to say, well, Super Mario Bros.

02:16:04.000 --> 02:16:05.600
3 is in, so Super Mario Bros.

02:16:05.720 --> 02:16:07.360
1 is out of the Essential games list.

02:16:07.500 --> 02:16:08.580
It's like, probably won't happen.

02:16:08.940 --> 02:16:14.700
So is there potential for Mega Man 1 and 2 to both be on the Essential games list?

02:16:14.880 --> 02:16:19.960
Sure. Is there more potential that I put Mega Man 1 on the Essential games list today,

02:16:20.440 --> 02:16:25.100
and then once 2 comes out, I say, guys, we've got to really evaluate that Mega Man 1 pick,

02:16:25.120 --> 02:16:27.460
because this is what a really Essential game looks like.

02:16:27.460 --> 02:16:34.740
Like, do we just look at the 80-something, the 86, 87 games that we've played so far,

02:16:34.740 --> 02:16:38.360
and just say, well, based on these, this is how we look at Essential games?

02:16:38.520 --> 02:16:43.000
Or do we look at them today, like, you know, in 2019, and say if they're Essential?

02:16:43.460 --> 02:16:48.580
It's very tough for me to say, and that's why I'm actually going to put it on the Essential games list.

02:16:48.580 --> 02:16:51.980
First off, I just want to say I do think this game is better than Metroid, hands down.

02:16:53.320 --> 02:16:54.520
I agree. I agree, too.

02:16:54.700 --> 02:16:59.220
If you're asking me, like, is this real, like, you know, would you really vote this one Essential?

02:16:59.220 --> 02:17:01.360
I think it's in my top five games of the year, no doubt,

02:17:01.380 --> 02:17:06.100
and probably up there in, like, my total games I've played so far on the NES.

02:17:07.100 --> 02:17:08.320
Will there be better games?

02:17:08.440 --> 02:17:11.640
Sure. But I'm going to just take it in the context of what we have currently,

02:17:11.640 --> 02:17:17.180
and this game is so unique and special right now in 1987's NES library

02:17:17.180 --> 02:17:21.640
that I have to put it on the Essential games list, not caring what comes later,

02:17:21.860 --> 02:17:26.160
because right currently, what we're offering you on the Essential games list,

02:17:26.200 --> 02:17:30.180
if you're following along with us, there's nothing else like that currently on there,

02:17:30.220 --> 02:17:32.360
so you need to have this game in there.

02:17:32.480 --> 02:17:34.520
You need that game right now on the Essential games list,

02:17:34.560 --> 02:17:40.860
and I fully do expect myself to have this off the Essential games list by the time the podcast is over.

02:17:40.860 --> 02:17:42.140
You know? So weird.

02:17:42.420 --> 02:17:46.300
But I think, as of right now, we need a Mega Man game on there,

02:17:46.340 --> 02:17:49.360
and I had such a good time with a game that was so difficult

02:17:49.360 --> 02:17:51.960
and really made me question my video game skills,

02:17:52.000 --> 02:17:57.580
that just for that alone, a game that is so tough that you still keep coming back to is a good game.

02:17:57.760 --> 02:17:58.700
You know? Yeah, exactly.

02:17:58.900 --> 02:18:00.820
You're the problem in this game.

02:18:01.020 --> 02:18:02.760
The game is not the problem here,

02:18:02.800 --> 02:18:05.420
and there are some weird things that I didn't like.

02:18:05.460 --> 02:18:09.980
I called out the respawning of enemies when you move slightly off the screen.

02:18:09.980 --> 02:18:12.360
I hate that because the game is about enemy placement,

02:18:12.440 --> 02:18:17.160
and so to just defeat an enemy but walk off the screen and spawn a new one seems cheap.

02:18:17.420 --> 02:18:18.920
I also don't like...

02:18:18.920 --> 02:18:20.400
Ladders. What's that?

02:18:20.440 --> 02:18:21.640
I'm sorry. You don't like ladders.

02:18:21.820 --> 02:18:23.040
Ladders. Yeah, I don't like... Well, it's true.

02:18:23.140 --> 02:18:24.660
I don't like the ladders in the game,

02:18:24.700 --> 02:18:27.400
and I felt that there might be a better way to deal with that.

02:18:27.740 --> 02:18:32.740
I also don't feel like the weapons are entirely balanced, the power-ups.

02:18:32.740 --> 02:18:36.660
I feel like I rarely used Gutsman unless I saw a boulder and said,

02:18:36.700 --> 02:18:38.220
this seems like a good time to use it.

02:18:38.280 --> 02:18:39.340
I never really thought about it.

02:18:39.560 --> 02:18:40.900
I didn't use it sometimes when I did see a boulder.

02:18:41.020 --> 02:18:41.840
Right, but that's true, too.

02:18:41.840 --> 02:18:48.540
I didn't use it then either. I also didn't really care for Iceman's ability other than to freeze the lava,

02:18:48.540 --> 02:18:50.800
which was necessary in that one particular stage.

02:18:51.180 --> 02:18:52.840
I didn't care for a lot of the bomb thing.

02:18:52.840 --> 02:18:54.900
I barely use that other than I'm big guys.

02:18:55.060 --> 02:18:56.600
But those are when you're supposed to use them.

02:18:56.600 --> 02:19:02.040
I just think there's a lot of things that Mega Man 2 can hopefully do better

02:19:02.040 --> 02:19:03.780
and things I'm looking forward to.

02:19:03.980 --> 02:19:06.660
But I definitely think as of right now, what I've played,

02:19:06.880 --> 02:19:09.840
this is an essential game and needs to be on our essential games list.

02:19:10.500 --> 02:19:11.600
There it is. So there you have it.

02:19:12.360 --> 02:19:13.420
That's a unanimous.

02:19:13.560 --> 02:19:14.780
It's a unanimous one.

02:19:15.620 --> 02:19:18.820
Our last unanimous one was like a while ago.

02:19:19.420 --> 02:19:20.820
Yeah. Probably Punch-Out.

02:19:20.860 --> 02:19:22.480
Punch-Out of Legend of Zelda. Oh, Punch-Out.

02:19:22.620 --> 02:19:23.480
Punch-Out, Punch-Out. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

02:19:23.580 --> 02:19:24.900
We definitely get Punch-Out unanimous.

02:19:24.900 --> 02:19:25.820
So it wasn't a while ago.

02:19:25.820 --> 02:19:28.020
We just love Nintendo games.

02:19:31.660 --> 02:19:34.300
That's the slogan, the catchphrase.

02:19:34.680 --> 02:19:35.780
Yeah. Mega Man.

02:19:35.980 --> 02:19:37.720
How about it, guys? Go Bills.

02:19:38.000 --> 02:19:39.480
Would you play an Ultraman game?

02:19:39.600 --> 02:19:42.420
Because that came out on Super Nintendo and isn't nearly as fun.

02:19:42.620 --> 02:19:43.660
Is that a real thing?

02:19:43.900 --> 02:19:46.060
Ultraman, yeah. It wasn't Mega Man series.

02:19:46.200 --> 02:19:47.060
It was Ultraman.

02:19:47.360 --> 02:19:48.420
You guys don't know Ultraman?

02:19:48.420 --> 02:19:49.360
Oh, yeah, Ultraman. I know.

02:19:49.380 --> 02:19:51.020
In the Godzilla Kaiju universe?

02:19:51.380 --> 02:19:52.740
No. Yeah. Okay.

02:19:52.960 --> 02:19:54.780
In the Kaiju-verse, Ultraman's around.

02:19:54.780 --> 02:19:55.860
So that's cool.

02:19:56.200 --> 02:19:57.620
Hopefully we get an Ultraman movie, though.

02:19:57.880 --> 02:19:59.080
Yeah. All for that stuff.

02:19:59.360 --> 02:20:00.540
Ultraman. I've been waiting for that.

02:20:01.120 --> 02:20:02.500
Sean always talks about Ultraman.

02:20:02.520 --> 02:20:08.440
I fell asleep 118 times during the original Godzilla 2015 movie.

02:20:08.560 --> 02:20:09.860
Yeah. That was a sleepy time.

02:20:10.240 --> 02:20:11.040
But we both slept.

02:20:11.260 --> 02:20:12.180
You know what's a good thing to do?

02:20:12.280 --> 02:20:13.120
Thanks for the invite.

02:20:13.120 --> 02:20:15.420
After a 2014 godly...

02:20:16.200 --> 02:20:17.440
All right. We're getting into the post-show territory.

02:20:17.620 --> 02:20:20.440
Let me finish the show. Thank you so much for listening to Nostalgia.

02:20:20.820 --> 02:20:23.660
You can join us again next week when we cover Wizards and Warriors.

02:20:24.140 --> 02:20:28.760
And two weeks from now, we'll be covering our best of 1987, where we're going to get

02:20:28.760 --> 02:20:30.660
to the bottom of what an essential game is.

02:20:30.740 --> 02:20:32.080
And maybe some of these come off.

02:20:32.140 --> 02:20:34.460
Maybe some of them do not come off.

02:20:34.600 --> 02:20:37.020
Maybe we rethink how we count the votes.

02:20:37.040 --> 02:20:40.840
How we count the votes. We're always going to be struggling with what an essential game is.

02:20:40.840 --> 02:20:43.080
Oh, yeah. It's an essential part of the podcast.

02:20:43.220 --> 02:20:47.220
Yeah. Our essential games are not mandatory for you to agree with.

02:20:47.220 --> 02:20:50.880
And we love hearing whether you do or don't agree with them.

02:20:51.020 --> 02:20:56.600
So tell us how you feel about Mega Man by writing to us at nostalgacast at gmail.com or

02:20:56.600 --> 02:20:59.700
just talking to us on Twitter at NostalgiaCast.

02:20:59.840 --> 02:21:02.600
I feel like that's been a very good way to get a hold of people.

02:21:03.200 --> 02:21:06.340
A lot of people have written into us on Twitter and let us know thoughts about games, which

02:21:06.340 --> 02:21:07.440
we're always happy to hear about.

02:21:07.440 --> 02:21:09.120
